In article <1993Jun3.092516.14196@indirect.com>, garbled@indirect.com (Tim Rightnour) writes:
 > I propose, that you use a numbering system.  A killer bee or xan would
 > have a number of one.  and for example an umber hulk a 10.
 > now, if a bee was directly behind the umber hulk.. you could never see it.
 >  However, A bee could block out an umber hulk 10 spaces away, due to the
 > fact that objects appear smaller when far away.  Therefore..  You would
 > need a simple algorithm, such as:
 > 
 > 1st monster # + distance - 2nd monster #.
 > then you would just look at the value,  if the number is 0 or below, the
 > monster is invisible.  1 or more, and you can see it.
 > 
 > I think that would be MUCH more realistic.
You would probably want to use distance2 (distance squared, for those of
you without iso-8859-1) as the size of things decrease with the square
of the distance.  The formula must take into account both the distance
to monster1 and monster 2.  A goblin 1 square away may block a dragon
20 squares away, but a goblin 40 squares away may not block a dragon 60 squares
away.
This kind of blocking should be based on chance, rather than either seeing or not.
You may now and then catch a glimpse of an advancing goblin even though you are 
busy fighting a large unicorn.
Different sized monsters give more opportunities for funny messages when
fighting.
You step on the rabid rat!
The rabid rat bites your leg!  
The rothe bites your arm!
The AC bonus for gloves would of course not affect a monster attacking
your leg and so on.
The chance of cutting off a head with Vorpal blade should be smaller
with a much larger monster, as its head would be too high up.  Instead you might
cut off a leg making the monster slower and lower.

The objects (bags, knifes, potionc...) should also
have a size attribute.  Darts are small, chests larger, boulders are
huge and block anything.  Statues have the same size as the corresponding 
monster.  An object a lot smaller than you won't block your sight, as you
see over it.  So you will still see most of the items in a shop from the
doorway, but not if you happen to be a grid bug at the moment, in wich case
one dart may block another.

